findings.message:
(minor) This -EAGAIN feeds the caller's generic failure arm in lov_io_ec_rd_start():

    if (rc) {
        CWARN("...fail to read stripe %d: rc = %d\n", ...);
        io->u.ci_ec.ec_recovery_failed = true;
        GOTO(rg_cleanup, rc);
    }

So losing the race with another reader is logged unconditionally on the console and recorded as an unrecoverable recovery failure, which also makes vvp_io_ec_rd_end() throw away the reconstruction already done for earlier recovery groups in this iteration.

By the reasoning in the comment above the retry loop, that is the expected outcome rather than a rare one: the owner can take up to obd_timeout coming back from a dead OST while the budget here caps out at 2s, so a second reader will normally exhaust it and restart. With four concurrent readers (sanity-ec test_74a) that is a steady stream of console warnings for a benign condition. Would it be worth distinguishing the contention -EAGAIN from a real stripe failure at the caller?

(defect) The match path falls into `reset:`, which does `*pid = 0`. `sa_pattern_fname_detect()` passes `&lli->lli_stat_pid` as `pid`, so a successful non-shared FNAME match now zeroes the directory statahead owner token; the old code left it at `current->pid`.

That disables the throttle at the end of `start_statahead_thread()`:

    if (lli->lli_stat_pid == current->pid)
            lli->lli_sa_enabled = 0;

With `lli_stat_pid == 0` it never fires, so an `-EMFILE` from the `ll_sa_running_max` check (or an `-ENOMEM` on the sai) no longer disables statahead for the dir. `lli_sa_pattern` keeps `LSA_PATTERN_FNAME`, `dentry_may_statahead()` keeps returning true, and the client re-attempts the failed thread creation every `statahead_fname_match_hit` stats instead of backing off once.

`sa_pattern_list_precheck()` also reads `lli_stat_pid == 0` as "no owner". Should the pid only be cleared for the shared-cache caller?

(minor) This is exactly lustre_msg_hdr_size_v2() from pack_generic.c:

    return round_up(offsetof(struct lustre_msg_v2, lm_buflens[count]), 8);

Open-coding it here means the two copies can drift if the header layout ever changes. lustre_msg_hdr_size() is the public wrapper but is not EXPORT_SYMBOL'd, so ptlrpc_gss cannot link against it today - would it be worth exporting it (or moving the _v2 helper into a header) and calling it from here?
